    "thm-tdmath-stable-tempered-free-cube-grand-pressure": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-stable-power-tempered-particle-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a positive integer $d$ and use the Euclidean space $\\mathbb R^d$ with norm\n$\\|x\\|_2=\\sqrt{\\langle x,x\\rangle}$ of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. A\npair potential is an even Borel-measurable function\n$\\phi:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ whose image is contained in\n$\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$. Here $\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ is the extended real\nline ([[def-extended-reals]]). Borel measurability means the inverse image of\neach Borel subset of $\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ belongs to the Borel sigma-algebra\ngenerated by open sets in the Euclidean topology on $\\mathbb R^d$\n([[def-borel-sigma-algebra-on-the-extended-real-line]],\n[[def-borel-sigma-algebra]]). Even means $\\phi(-x)=\\phi(x)$. The value\n$+\\infty$ represents a forbidden pair separation. Coordinates and\npotential values are numerical values in fixed reference length and energy\nunits.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-particle-exponential-cutoff",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $d\\ge1$, let $\\phi$ be a stable pair\npotential as in [[def-tdmath-stable-power-tempered-particle-model]] with\nstability constant $B$, and fix $a,\\beta>0$. In fixed reference length and\nenergy units, put $\\Lambda_L=[0,L]^d$ and define, for $N\\ge1$,\n$$Z_{L,N}:=\\frac{a^N}{N!}\\int_{\\Lambda_L^N}e^{-\\beta U_N(q_1,\\ldots,q_N)}\\,d\\lambda_d^{\\otimes N},$$\nwhere $\\lambda_d$ is Lebesgue measure restricted to the Borel subsets of\n$\\mathbb R^d$, $\\lambda_d^{\\otimes N}$ is its $N$-fold product measure. The\nintegrand uses the ordinary real exponential when $U_N<+\\infty$ and is defined\nto be $0$ when $U_N=+\\infty$. Also,\n$\\int_{\\Lambda_L^N}f\\,d\\lambda_d^{\\otimes N}$ abbreviates the nonnegative\nintegral of $f\\mathbf1_{\\Lambda_L^N}$ on the full product space, using\n[[def-tdmath-measure-integral]]. Set $Z_{L,0}=1$.\nDefine $\\Xi_L:=\\sum_{N=0}^{\\infty}Z_{L,N}$ as the nonnegative extended sum of\n[[def-nonnegative-extended-series]]. For $L\\ge1$ put\n$Q:=a e^{\\beta B}$. Choose any real $K>eQ+1$ and set\n$$n_L:=\\lfloor K L^d\\rfloor+1.$$\nwhich satisfies $K L^d<n_L\\le K L^d+1$ by the integer-part lemma\n[[lem-integer-part]]. Let\n$\\Xi_L^{\\mathrm{cut}}:=\\sum_{N=0}^{n_L}Z_{L,N}$. Then\n$$1\\le\\Xi_L\\le e^{Q L^d},\\qquad \\sum_{N>n_L}Z_{L,N}\\le e^{-L^d},\\qquad \\Xi_L^{\\mathrm{cut}}\\ge(1-e^{-L^d})\\Xi_L.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-separated-cube-tail-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$ and let $\\phi$ be power-tempered as in\n[[def-tdmath-stable-power-tempered-particle-model]], with constants\n$C,R,\\epsilon>0$. Define\n$$S_{d,\\epsilon}:=\\sum_{m=1}^{\\infty}\\bigl((2m+1)^d-(2m-1)^d\\bigr)m^{-d-\\epsilon},\\qquad \\|k\\|_\\infty:=\\max_{1\\le r\\le d}|k_r| \\quad(k\\in\\mathbb Z^d).$$\nThis nonnegative series, defined by [[def-nonnegative-extended-series]],\ngroups the lattice terms by shells $\\|k\\|_\\infty=m$.\nThen $S_{d,\\epsilon}<\\infty$. Let $\\ell>0$, $g\\ge R$, and let\n$J\\subset\\mathbb Z^d$ be finite with $M=|J|$. For each $j\\in J$, place at most\n$n$ particles in the cube $C_j=(\\ell+g)j+[0,\\ell]^d$, where $n$ is a\nnonnegative integer. The total absolute inter-cube energy satisfies\n$$\\sum_{\\substack{j,j'\\in J\\;j<j'}}\\;\\sum_{q\\in C_j}\\sum_{q'\\in C_{j'}}|\\phi(q-q')|\\le\\frac C2 M n^2 S_{d,\\epsilon}g^{-d-\\epsilon}.$$\nHere $j<j'$ means any fixed total ordering of the finite set $J$; each\nunordered pair of cubes is counted once.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-tdmath-measure-integral",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A measure space is a triple $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ where $\\Sigma$ contains $\\varnothing,X$ and is closed under complements and countable unions (a $\\sigma$-algebra) of subsets of $X$ and $\\mu:\\Sigma\\to[0,\\infty]$ is countably additive with $\\mu(\\varnothing)=0$. A measurable function $f:X\\to[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has measurable inverse images of Borel sets, where $[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has its order topology: finite open intervals and the endpoint neighborhoods $[-\\infty,a)$ and $(a,\\infty]$ form a basis. Its Borel sigma-algebra is generated by these open sets. A complex-valued function is measurable exactly when its finite real and imaginary parts are measurable, equivalently for the usual Borel structure of $\\mathbb C$. Almost-everywhere assertions mean outside a measurable $\\mu$-null set; completeness of $\\mu$ is not assumed. Nonnegative extended addition uses $a+\\infty=\\infty+a=\\infty$ for $a\\in[0,\\infty]$; positive scalars multiply $\\infty$ to $\\infty$, while $0\\cdot\\infty=0$. Signed expressions $\\infty-\\infty$ remain undefined and are not used in signed integrals. A nonnegative simple function has the form $s=\\sum_{j=1}^m c_j\\mathbf1_{A_j}$ with finitely many disjoint $A_j\\in\\Sigma$ and finite $c_j\\ge0$. Define $c\\mu(A)=+\\infty$ if $c>0$ and $\\mu(A)=+\\infty$, define $0\\cdot\\infty:=0$, and define the finite sum of these extended nonnegative terms to be $+\\infty$ if any term is $+\\infty$ and otherwise the real finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]]. Then $\\int s\\,d\\mu:=\\sum_j c_j\\mu(A_j)$; using canonical level sets $\\{s=c\\}$ makes this value representation-independent. For measurable $f\\ge0$, define $\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\sup\\{\\int s\\,d\\mu: s\\text{ is simple and }0\\le s\\le f\\}$, using [[def-complete-ordered-field]] for a bounded set of finite values and setting the supremum to $+\\infty$ when the values are unbounded or one is infinite. A real measurable $f$ is integrable when $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$, and then $\\int f:=\\int f^+-\\int f^-$, where $f^+=\\max(f,0)$ and $f^-:=\\max(-f,0)$. Complex integrability and integration are defined by integrable real and imaginary parts.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-measure-integral-agrees-with-root-lebesgue-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let $f:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be\n$\\Sigma$-measurable. The nonnegative integral defined by\n[[def-tdmath-measure-integral]] equals the shared nonnegative Lebesgue integral\ndefined by [[def-nonnegative-lebesgue-integral]], with the same value in\n$[0,+\\infty]$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-borel-products-of-euclidean-spaces-are-euclidean-borel",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m,n \\ge 1$. Under the identification\n$$\\mathbb R^{m+n}=\\mathbb R^m \\times \\mathbb R^n,$$\none has\n$$\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^m)\\otimes\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^n) = \\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^{m+n}).$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces. Then:",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
