        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-real-power",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $a>0$ and $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$a^x:=\\exp(x\\log a).$$\nFor the supplementary zero-base convention, define $0^x:=0$ when $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-real-power-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $a,b>0$ and $r,s\\in\\mathbb R$,\n$$a^{r+s}=a^ra^s,\\qquad (ab)^r=a^rb^r,\\qquad (a/b)^r=a^r/b^r,\\qquad (a^r)^s=a^{rs}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $a>0$, the function $x\\mapsto a^x$ is continuous on $\\mathbb R$ and\n$$(a^x)'=a^x\\log a.$$\nFor $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R$, the function $x\\mapsto x^\\alpha$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty)$, with\n$$(x^\\alpha)'=\\alpha x^{\\alpha-1}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-exponential-limits-and-range",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$$\\exp(x)\\to+\\infty\\quad(x\\to+\\infty),\\qquad \\exp(x)\\to0\\quad(x\\to-\\infty),$$\nand the range of $\\exp$ is contained in $(0,\\infty)$ and is unbounded above with infimum $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "lem-integer-part",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Identify $\\mathbb{Z}$ with its canonical copy inside $\\mathbb{R}$, along the\nembeddings $\\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{Q} \\to \\mathbb{R}$\n([[lem-nat-embeds-int]], [[lem-int-embeds-rat]], [[lem-rat-embeds-dense]],\n[[def-integers]]). Then for every real $x$ there is **exactly one** integer $m$\nwith",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "def-factorial-and-falling-factorial",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**The factorial.** By the recursion theorem ([[thm-recursion]]) applied to the\nset $\\mathbb{N}\\times\\mathbb{N}$, the starting element $(0,1)$ and the function\n$f(k, v) = (\\sigma(k),\\, v \\cdot \\sigma(k))$, and by the same induction on the\nfirst coordinate as in [[def-nat-finite-sum-and-product]], there is a unique\n$F : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ with",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Set $\\alpha:=\\frac12\\bigl(1+d/(d+\\epsilon)\\bigr)$ and\n$g_\\ell:=\\ell^\\alpha$. Then $d/(d+\\epsilon)<\\alpha<1$ and\n$\\ell^d g_\\ell^{-d-\\epsilon}=\\ell^{d-\\alpha(d+\\epsilon)}\\to0$ while\n$g_\\ell/\\ell\\to0$. Fix a sufficiently large $\\ell\\ge1$ so that\n$g_\\ell\\ge R$. For $L\\ge\\ell+g_\\ell$, set\n$m_L:=\\lfloor L/(\\ell+g_\\ell)\\rfloor$. Since $L/(\\ell+g_\\ell)\\ge1$, [F10]\ngives $m_L\\ge1$ and\n$m_L(\\ell+g_\\ell)\\le L<(m_L+1)(\\ell+g_\\ell)$. Place $M_L=m_L^d$ cubes\n$$C_j=(\\ell+g_\\ell)j+[0,\\ell]^d,\\qquad j\\in\\{0,\\ldots,m_L-1\\}^d.$$\nThey lie inside $[0,L]^d$ and have pairwise gaps at least $g_\\ell$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Fix occupancies $0\\le N_j\\le n_\\ell$ with total $N=\\sum_jN_j$. The\nordered configurations in the disjoint cubes with exactly these occupancies\nare partitioned by assignments of the $N$ particle labels to cubes. The number\nof assignments is $N!/(\\prod_jN_j!)$: order all $N$ labels, then note that\neach assignment occurs exactly $\\prod_jN_j!$ times from within-cube reorderings.\nParticle-permutation symmetry of $U_N$ and invariance of the finite product\nmeasure (rectangle formula and uniqueness in [F6]) make the assignment\nintegrals equal.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F6",
            "F11",
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Restrict the grand partition sum in $[0,L]^d$ to configurations with no\nparticles outside these cubes and at most $n_\\ell$ particles in each cube,\nwhere $n_\\ell$ is the cutoff from [F2]. If two cubes have index difference\n$k\\ne0$, the coordinate gap in a coordinate attaining $\\|k\\|_\\infty$ is at\nleast $\\|k\\|_\\infty g_\\ell$; [F3] therefore bounds the total absolute\ninter-cube energy by\n$$D_L\\le\\frac C2M_Ln_\\ell^2S_{d,\\epsilon}g_\\ell^{-d-\\epsilon}.$$\nThus $U_N=\\sum_jU_{N_j}^{(j)}+U_{\\mathrm{cross}}$ with\n$U_{\\mathrm{cross}}\\le D_L$, and\n$e^{-\\beta U_N}\\ge e^{-\\beta D_L}\\prod_j e^{-\\beta U_{N_j}^{(j)}}$;\nthis remains true when an internal energy is $+\\infty$, since both sides then\nvanish.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "On each assignment domain from step 2.1, the pointwise lower bound in\nstep 2.2 factors into nonnegative within-cube integrands. Repeated application\nof [F5] and the rectangle formula in [F6] factors their integral into the\nproduct of the translated one-cube integrals; [F7] identifies each with its\norigin-centered value. Multiplying by $a^N/N!$ cancels the label count from\nstep 2.1, leaving $e^{-\\beta D_L}\\prod_j Z_{\\ell,N_j}$. Summing over the\nfinitely many occupancy vectors and expanding the finite product of cutoff\nsums from [F2] gives\n$$\\Xi_L\\ge e^{-\\beta D_L}(\\Xi_\\ell^{\\mathrm{cut}})^{M_L}.$$",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The integer-part bounds in step 1.1 give\n$L/(\\ell+g_\\ell)-1<m_L\\le L/(\\ell+g_\\ell)$, so\n$m_L/L\\to(\\ell+g_\\ell)^{-1}$ and $M_L/L^d\\to(\\ell+g_\\ell)^{-d}$.\nBy [F2], $\\Xi_\\ell^{\\mathrm{cut}}\\ge(1-e^{-\\ell^d})\\Xi_\\ell$, so\n$\\log\\Xi_\\ell^{\\mathrm{cut}}\\ge\\ell^dp_\\ell+\\log(1-e^{-\\ell^d})$.\nTaking logarithms in step 3.1, dividing by $L^d$, and using step 2.2 gives\n$$p_L\\ge\\frac{M_L}{L^d}\\left(\\ell^dp_\\ell+\\log(1-e^{-\\ell^d})-\\frac{\\beta C}{2}n_\\ell^2S_{d,\\epsilon}g_\\ell^{-d-\\epsilon}\\right).$$\nTaking the lower limit and using the volume ratio above yields the same bound\nwith $M_L/L^d$ replaced by $(\\ell+g_\\ell)^{-d}$. The cutoff definition [F2]\nand $\\ell\\ge1$ give\n$n_\\ell\\le K\\ell^d+1\\le(K+1)\\ell^d$. Factoring\n$(\\ell/(\\ell+g_\\ell))^d$ and using this estimate in the negative error term\ntherefore gives\n$$\\liminf_{L\\to\\infty}p_L\\ge\\left(\\frac{\\ell}{\\ell+g_\\ell}\\right)^d\\left[p_\\ell+\\ell^{-d}\\log(1-e^{-\\ell^d})-\\frac{\\beta C}{2}(K+1)^2S_{d,\\epsilon}\\ell^dg_\\ell^{-d-\\epsilon}\\right].$$",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F9",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "As $\\ell\\to\\infty$, the prefactor tends to $1$. Also\n$e^{-\\ell^d}\\to0$, so continuity of $\\log$ at $1$ gives\n$\\log(1-e^{-\\ell^d})\\to0$; since $\\ell^{-d}\\to0$, their product\n$\\ell^{-d}\\log(1-e^{-\\ell^d})$ tends to zero. The cross-energy error tends to\nzero because $d-\\alpha(d+\\epsilon)<0$ by step 1.1. The replacement of the\nprefactor times $p_\\ell$ by $p_\\ell$ has error at most\n$Q\\bigl[1-(\\ell/(\\ell+g_\\ell))^d\\bigr]\\to0$ by [F2]. Thus for every\n$\\eta>0$ there is $T$ such that every $\\ell\\ge T$ makes the right side of\nstep 4.1 at least $p_\\ell-\\eta$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F9",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "Let $\\overline p=\\limsup_{\\ell\\to\\infty}p_\\ell$. By [A1], for every\n$\\eta>0$ and every $T\\ge1$ there is an $\\ell\\ge T$ with\n$p_\\ell>\\overline p-\\eta$; otherwise the tail supremum from $T$ onward would\nbe at most $\\overline p-\\eta$, contradicting the infimum defining\n$\\overline p$. Choose $T$ large enough for step 5.1. Then\n$\\liminf_{L\\to\\infty}p_L\\ge\\overline p-2\\eta$. Letting $\\eta\\downarrow0$\nshows $\\liminf p_L\\ge\\limsup p_L$.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 5.1",
            "choose",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-7-1",
          "claim": "For any $S,T\\ge1$, choose $L\\ge\\max(S,T)$. Then\n$\\inf_{x\\ge S}p_x\\le p_L\\le\\sup_{x\\ge T}p_x$. Taking the supremum over $S$\nand then the infimum over $T$ proves\n$\\liminf p_L\\le\\limsup p_L$ by [A1]. Together with step 6.1, the two finite\ntail bounds equal a number $p$. For every $\\eta>0$, their definitions give\ntail thresholds with $\\inf_{L\\ge T_-}p_L>p-\\eta$ and\n$\\sup_{L\\ge T_+}p_L<p+\\eta$; hence $|p_L-p|<\\eta$ for all sufficiently large\n$L$. The limit is finite by [F2]. This proves existence for the specified\nfree-cube reduced grand pressure.",
          "step": "7.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "step 6.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
