      "finite_checks": [],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-tdmath-stable-power-tempered-particle-model",
          "carrier": "items/def-tdmath-stable-power-tempered-particle-model.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix a positive integer $d$ and use the Euclidean space $\\mathbb R^d$ with norm\n$\\|x\\|_2=\\sqrt{\\langle x,x\\rangle}$ of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. A\npair potential is an even Borel-measurable function\n$\\phi:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ whose image is contained in\n$\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$. Here $\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ is the extended real\nline ([[def-extended-reals]]). Borel measurability means the inverse image of\neach Borel subset of $\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ belongs to the Borel sigma-algebra\ngenerated by open sets in the Euclidean topology on $\\mathbb R^d$\n([[def-borel-sigma-algebra-on-the-extended-real-line]],\n[[def-borel-sigma-algebra]]). Even means $\\phi(-x)=\\phi(x)$. The value\n$+\\infty$ represents a forbidden pair separation. Coordinates and\npotential values are numerical values in fixed reference length and energy\nunits.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact excerpt is quoted above; the consumer Facts and proof step state the hypotheses and conventions used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current draft definition carrier present; no proof obligation; interface inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 is used in step(s) 2.1, 2.2."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-tdmath-particle-exponential-cutoff",
          "carrier": "items/lem-tdmath-particle-exponential-cutoff.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $d\\ge1$, let $\\phi$ be a stable pair\npotential as in [[def-tdmath-stable-power-tempered-particle-model]] with\nstability constant $B$, and fix $a,\\beta>0$. In fixed reference length and\nenergy units, put $\\Lambda_L=[0,L]^d$ and define, for $N\\ge1$,\n$$Z_{L,N}:=\\frac{a^N}{N!}\\int_{\\Lambda_L^N}e^{-\\beta U_N(q_1,\\ldots,q_N)}\\,d\\lambda_d^{\\otimes N},$$\nwhere $\\lambda_d$ is Lebesgue measure restricted to the Borel subsets of\n$\\mathbb R^d$, $\\lambda_d^{\\otimes N}$ is its $N$-fold product measure. The\nintegrand uses the ordinary real exponential when $U_N<+\\infty$ and is defined\nto be $0$ when $U_N=+\\infty$. Also,\n$\\int_{\\Lambda_L^N}f\\,d\\lambda_d^{\\otimes N}$ abbreviates the nonnegative\nintegral of $f\\mathbf1_{\\Lambda_L^N}$ on the full product space, using\n[[def-tdmath-measure-integral]]. Set $Z_{L,0}=1$.\nDefine $\\Xi_L:=\\sum_{N=0}^{\\infty}Z_{L,N}$ as the nonnegative extended sum of\n[[def-nonnegative-extended-series]]. For $L\\ge1$ put\n$Q:=a e^{\\beta B}$. Choose any real $K>eQ+1$ and set\n$$n_L:=\\lfloor K L^d\\rfloor+1.$$\nwhich satisfies $K L^d<n_L\\le K L^d+1$ by the integer-part lemma\n[[lem-integer-part]]. Let\n$\\Xi_L^{\\mathrm{cut}}:=\\sum_{N=0}^{n_L}Z_{L,N}$. Then\n$$1\\le\\Xi_L\\le e^{Q L^d},\\qquad \\sum_{N>n_L}Z_{L,N}\\le e^{-L^d},\\qquad \\Xi_L^{\\mathrm{cut}}\\ge(1-e^{-L^d})\\Xi_L.$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact excerpt is quoted above; the consumer Facts and proof step state the hypotheses and conventions used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current draft proof carrier present; independent review not asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 is used in step(s) 2.2, 3.1, 4.1, 5.1, 7.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-tdmath-separated-cube-tail-bound",
          "carrier": "items/lem-tdmath-separated-cube-tail-bound.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $d\\ge1$ and let $\\phi$ be power-tempered as in\n[[def-tdmath-stable-power-tempered-particle-model]], with constants\n$C,R,\\epsilon>0$. Define\n$$S_{d,\\epsilon}:=\\sum_{m=1}^{\\infty}\\bigl((2m+1)^d-(2m-1)^d\\bigr)m^{-d-\\epsilon},\\qquad \\|k\\|_\\infty:=\\max_{1\\le r\\le d}|k_r| \\quad(k\\in\\mathbb Z^d).$$\nThis nonnegative series, defined by [[def-nonnegative-extended-series]],\ngroups the lattice terms by shells $\\|k\\|_\\infty=m$.\nThen $S_{d,\\epsilon}<\\infty$. Let $\\ell>0$, $g\\ge R$, and let\n$J\\subset\\mathbb Z^d$ be finite with $M=|J|$. For each $j\\in J$, place at most\n$n$ particles in the cube $C_j=(\\ell+g)j+[0,\\ell]^d$, where $n$ is a\nnonnegative integer. The total absolute inter-cube energy satisfies\n$$\\sum_{\\substack{j,j'\\in J\\;j<j'}}\\;\\sum_{q\\in C_j}\\sum_{q'\\in C_{j'}}|\\phi(q-q')|\\le\\frac C2 M n^2 S_{d,\\epsilon}g^{-d-\\epsilon}.$$\nHere $j<j'$ means any fixed total ordering of the finite set $J$; each\nunordered pair of cubes is counted once.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact excerpt is quoted above; the consumer Facts and proof step state the hypotheses and conventions used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current draft proof carrier present; independent review not asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 is used in step(s) 2.2."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-tdmath-measure-integral-agrees-with-root-lebesgue-integral",
          "carrier": "items/lem-tdmath-measure-integral-agrees-with-root-lebesgue-integral.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let $f:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be\n$\\Sigma$-measurable. The nonnegative integral defined by\n[[def-tdmath-measure-integral]] equals the shared nonnegative Lebesgue integral\ndefined by [[def-nonnegative-lebesgue-integral]], with the same value in\n$[0,+\\infty]$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact excerpt is quoted above; the consumer Facts and proof step state the hypotheses and conventions used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current draft proof carrier present; independent review not asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in step(s) 3.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-tdmath-measure-integral",
          "carrier": "items/def-tdmath-measure-integral.md",
          "status": "current draft supplier; interface inspected, independent acceptance not asserted",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "A measure space is a triple $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ where $\\Sigma$ contains $\\varnothing,X$ and is closed under complements and countable unions (a $\\sigma$-algebra) of subsets of $X$ and $\\mu:\\Sigma\\to[0,\\infty]$ is countably additive with $\\mu(\\varnothing)=0$. A measurable function $f:X\\to[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has measurable inverse images of Borel sets, where the Borel sets of the extended real line are the sigma-algebra generated by its open sets. A nonnegative simple function has the form $s=\\sum_{j=1}^m c_j\\mathbf1_{A_j}$ with finitely many disjoint $A_j\\in\\Sigma$ and finite $c_j\\ge0$. Define $c\\mu(A)=+\\infty$ if $c>0$ and $\\mu(A)=+\\infty$, define $0\\cdot\\infty:=0$, and define the finite sum of these extended nonnegative terms to be $+\\infty$ if any term is $+\\infty$ and otherwise the real finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]]. Then $\\int s\\,d\\mu:=\\sum_j c_j\\mu(A_j)$; using canonical level sets $\\{s=c\\}$ makes this value representation-independent. For measurable $f\\ge0$, define $\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\sup\\{\\int s\\,d\\mu: s\\text{ is simple and }0\\le s\\le f\\}$, using [[def-complete-ordered-field]] for a bounded set of finite values and setting the supremum to $+\\infty$ when the values are unbounded or one is infinite. A real measurable $f$ is integrable when $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$, and then $\\int f:=\\int f^+-\\int f^-$, where $f^+=\\max(f,0)$ and $f^-:=\\max(-f,0)$. Complex integrability and integration are defined by integrable real and imaginary parts.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact excerpt is quoted above; the consumer Facts and proof step state the hypotheses and conventions used.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current draft definition carrier present; no proof obligation; interface inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in step(s) 3.1."
        },
