          "claim": "Put $\\delta:=1/(2\\sqrt d)$. For a fixed particle $q_i$, the half-open\ncubes $D_k:=q_i+\\delta k+[0,\\delta)^d$, $k\\in\\mathbb Z^d$, partition\n$\\mathbb R^d$. Their Euclidean diameters are at most $\\sqrt d\\,\\delta=1/2<1$.\nThe example's definition makes every pair at distance less than $1$ forbidden,\nso centers in a finite-energy configuration are separated by at least $1$.\nThus each cube contains at most one center and $D_0$ contains no center other\nthan $q_i$. The cells in max-norm shell $m$ number\n$(2m+1)^d-(2m-1)^d$ by [F3]. For $m\\ge1$, factor the difference of powers:\n$$(2m+1)^d-(2m-1)^d=2\\sum_{r=0}^{d-1}(2m+1)^{d-1-r}(2m-1)^r\\le2d3^{d-1}m^{d-1},$$\nsince $2m+1\\le3m$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "A neighbor of $q_i$ in shell $m=1$ has distance at least $1$ and\ncontributes at most $c$ in absolute value; there are $3^d-1$ such cells. For\n$m\\ge2$, choose a coordinate $r$ with $|k_r|=m$. If $k_r=m$, every point of\n$D_k$ differs from $q_i$ in coordinate $r$ by at least $\\delta m$; if\n$k_r=-m$, the absolute difference is greater than $\\delta(m-1)$. Hence every\npoint of $D_k$ is at Euclidean distance at least $\\delta(m-1)$ from $q_i$.\nBy [F2], each such neighbor contributes at most\n$c\\delta^{-p}(m-1)^{-p}$. The shell count in step 1.2 therefore bounds its\ntotal contribution by\n$$2d3^{d-1}m^{d-1}c\\delta^{-p}(m-1)^{-d-\\epsilon}.$$",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For $m\\ge2$, $m-1\\ge m/2$, so the last bound is at most\n$2d3^{d-1}c\\delta^{-p}2^p m^{-1-\\epsilon}$. Summing over shells and using\n[F4], every fixed particle satisfies\n$$\\sum_{j\\ne i}|\\phi_{\\mathrm{att}}(q_i-q_j)|\\le C_*:=c(3^d-1)+2d3^{d-1}c\\delta^{-p}2^p\\sum_{m=2}^{\\infty}m^{-1-\\epsilon}<\\infty.$$",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "For a finite-energy configuration, every pair term is negative and\n$$-U_N=\\sum_{i<j}|\\phi_{\\mathrm{att}}(q_i-q_j)|=\\frac12\\sum_i\\sum_{j\\ne i}|\\phi_{\\mathrm{att}}(q_i-q_j)|\\le\\frac{C_*}{2}N$$\nby step 3.1. A configuration containing a forbidden pair has $U_N=+\\infty$\nand also satisfies the lower bound. Hence the potential is stable with\n$B=C_*/2$. Together with step 1.1, this proves membership in the stated\nstable power-tempered class.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
